WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:01.733
This is Marina Beach today.

00:01.733 --> 00:04.617
It is one of the longest city beaches in the world, and one of

00:04.617 --> 00:08.360
the widest. On a busy evening there is room for thousands of people

00:08.360 --> 00:10.000
between the road and the water.

00:10.000 --> 00:11.587
Here is a question to hold on to.

00:11.587 --> 00:13.449
Has this beach always been here?

00:13.449 --> 00:15.806
Go back about one hundred and fifty years.

00:15.806 --> 00:19.156
This photograph was taken from the lighthouse in Madras, around

00:19.156 --> 00:21.365
eighteen seventy. Look at the shore.

00:21.365 --> 00:25.136
There is one long pier reaching into the sea, and no harbor walls

00:25.136 --> 00:27.908
at all. Ships had to anchor out in the open sea.

00:27.908 --> 00:30.079
By eighteen eighty, that was changing.

00:30.079 --> 00:32.751
This newspaper page shows the new harbor works.

00:32.751 --> 00:36.313
Engineers were casting huge concrete blocks and setting them in

00:36.313 --> 00:38.985
the sea to build long walls called breakwaters.

00:38.985 --> 00:41.155
The plan was to give ships calm water.

00:41.155 --> 00:43.271
Nobody was planning to build a beach.

00:43.271 --> 00:46.493
Thirty years later the harbor was working, and something else

00:46.493 --> 00:50.193
had happened. Along this coast, the waves move more sand toward

00:50.193 --> 00:52.036
the north than toward the south.

00:52.036 --> 00:53.951
The breakwaters stood in its way.

00:53.951 --> 00:57.443
The sand could not get past, so it began to pile up on the south

00:57.443 --> 00:58.524
side of the harbor.

00:58.524 --> 01:00.556
Here is the same coast from a satellite.

01:00.556 --> 01:01.764
North is at the top.

01:01.764 --> 01:04.324
At the bottom is Marina Beach, wide and pale.

01:04.324 --> 01:07.907
That sand is what the harbor has trapped over more than a century.

01:07.907 --> 01:09.434
Now look north of the port.

01:09.434 --> 01:12.746
The beach there is thin, and it is lined with short walls built

01:12.746 --> 01:14.121
to hold on to what is left.

01:14.121 --> 01:16.169
One side of the harbor gained land.

01:16.169 --> 01:17.604
The other side lost it.

01:17.604 --> 01:19.359
People are not the only force at work.

01:19.359 --> 01:23.091
About an hour down the coast is the Shore Temple at Mahabalipuram.

01:23.091 --> 01:26.661
It was built of granite about thirteen hundred years ago, right

01:26.661 --> 01:27.845
at the edge of the sea.

01:27.845 --> 01:31.298
Here it is in the early nineteen hundreds, with sand piled against

01:31.298 --> 01:34.684
its walls and fallen blocks around it, and here it is this year.

01:34.684 --> 01:37.708
The temple still stands, but look closely at the stone.

01:37.708 --> 01:40.621
Edges that were carved sharp are rounded now.

01:40.621 --> 01:42.852
Details are soft and blurred.

01:42.852 --> 01:46.903
Salt spray, wind, rain and waves have been wearing this rock down,

01:46.903 --> 01:49.766
grain by grain, for more than a thousand years.

01:49.766 --> 01:51.737
So the coast you know is not fixed.

01:51.737 --> 01:54.897
A beach can grow. A shore can disappear.

01:54.897 --> 01:57.011
Solid granite can slowly wear away.

01:57.011 --> 01:59.489
In this unit you will learn to read those changes.

01:59.489 --> 02:02.544
For any place, you will ask three questions.

02:02.544 --> 02:04.066
What broke the rock down?

02:04.066 --> 02:05.381
What moved the pieces?

02:05.381 --> 02:07.060
And where did they end up?

02:07.060 --> 02:11.013
By the end, you will choose one real place near Chennai and tell

02:11.013 --> 02:14.607
its story: how it was made, what is shaping it now, and what it

02:14.607 --> 02:16.234
could look like fifty years from today.
